    "contact_source_ids": ["s1"],
    "description": "Rightel is an Iranian mobile telecommunications company associated with the country’s third mobile-operator licence and the introduction of third-generation mobile service. Its company profile identifies it as a subsidiary of the Social Security Investment Company.",
    "tags": ["Rightel", "Mobile networks", "3G", "Telecommunications", "Social Security Investment Company"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T09:16:04Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T09:16:04Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Rightel: Iran’s Third Mobile Operator and 3G Origins | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Rightel’s licensing background, early 3G plans, investment-company connection, and documented internet disruptions."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "identity-and-ownership",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A third-operator identity",
        "content": "Rightel, also styled RighTel, is an Iranian telecommunications company whose public profile identifies it with the country’s third mobile-operator licence. The profile gives 2007 as its establishment year and identifies the company as a subsidiary of the Social Security Investment Company, itself the investment company of the Social Security Organization. It lists Tehran as its headquarters. These are company-reported details rather than a newly verified shareholder register. [s1]\n\nThe dates attached to Rightel require care. Establishing a company, obtaining a licence, announcing a brand and making service commercially available are separate milestones. A founding year alone cannot establish when customers first obtained working mobile service. The available sources describe different stages of that development. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "early-3g-plans",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The early promise of 3G",
        "content": "In November 2011, the industry publication MEED reported that Tamin Telecom planned to launch service under the Rightel brand in February 2012. Its report presented the network as Iran’s first third-generation mobile offering. Third-generation service, usually abbreviated to 3G, was central to the operator’s proposed distinction from established competitors. [s2]\n\nMEED also described a complicated licensing history involving an earlier partnership with the United Arab Emirates operator Etisalat, cancellation of that arrangement, and Tamin Telecom’s subsequent recovery of the licence. Its account linked launch delays to funding and changes in the board. These details describe the project’s reported circumstances at the time; they do not independently establish the eventual launch date or the completion of rollout targets. [s2]\n\nThe article’s coverage targets were forward-looking. They should therefore remain part of the history of Rightel’s plans, rather than being repeated as achieved coverage. The same caution applies to early expectations about the duration of exclusive access to 3G: a contemporaneous forecast requires later regulatory evidence before it can become a definitive chronology. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "access-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A network within a shared internet environment",
        "content": "Rightel appeared alongside MCI and Irancell in independent measurements of repeated internet outages during September–October 2022. Multiple measurement systems corroborated disruptions on the mobile networks. This provides evidence about historical access conditions, but does not identify every internal decision or technical mechanism at Rightel. Nor does it establish present availability or comparative service quality. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-significance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to understand Rightel’s role",
        "content": "Rightel’s early significance lies in the connection between a new operator licence and the proposed introduction of a new generation of mobile service. Its investment-company affiliation is another distinctive part of its institutional background. A complete assessment of its later development needs dated evidence on operating scale, network expansion and ownership, beyond the early plans and company description inspected here. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
